      "text": "To determine how many books Melvin has left over after repacking them into boxes of 40, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of books:**\n   Each box contains 39 books, and there are 1335 boxes. Therefore, the total number of books is:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39\n   \\]\n\n2. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   To find the total number of books, we multiply 1335 by 39:\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39 = 1335 \\times (40 - 1) = 1335 \\times 40 - 1335 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   Calculate \\(1335 \\times 40\\):\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 40 = 53400\n   \\]\n   Calculate \\(1335 \\times 1\\):\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 1 = 1335\n   \\]\n   Subtract the second product from the first:\n   \\[\n   53400 - 1335 = 52065\n   \\]\n   So, the total number of books is:\n   \\[\n   52065\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine how many books are left over:**\n   We need to repack the 52065 books into boxes of 40 books each. To find the number of books left over, we take the total number of books modulo 40:\n   \\[\n   52065 \\div 40\n   \\]\n   Perform the division to find the quotient and the remainder:\n   \\[\n   52065 \\div 40 = 1301 \\text{ R } 25\n   \\]\n   This means that 52065 divided by 40 gives a quotient of 1301 and a remainder of 25. Therefore, the number of books left over is:\n   \\[\n   25\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of books left over is \\(\\boxed{25}\\).",
